A||||15||Uaana||I'm cold||||8||
Z||000000||Uaana||01-23-2000||01:03 AM||||Hello all.<BR>I have an 87 GT and as opposed to my old<BR>85 SE I can't get it warm inside.<BR>It takes almost 10-15 min for the idle to kick down. and I cant keep the temp warm inside if the blower is going. Not to mention<BR>the the temp drops at hwy speeds.<BR>BTW the temp has been around the teens and below.  Any suggestions?||||reg||8
Z||000001||Phaeton||01-23-2000||02:01 AM||||Take out the thermostat and see if it is sealed shut, if yes then put it in a pan of water on the stove and see how hot it is when it opens.  If you do not have a thermometer use your finger, if it opens and you can hold your finger in the water then throw it away.  I'm assuming the radiator fan is not running constantly.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Patrick||01-23-2000||02:51 AM||||<BR>It's not a problem of the thermostat being sealed shut, it's a problem of the thermostat being stuck open. I had a Chev that would take forever to get warm, and that's what the problem was.<P>Phaeton, that's probably what you meant, but it just didn't sound right. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>||||reg||4
Z||000003||batboy||01-23-2000||02:52 AM||||Your thermostat is probably stuck part way open, it happens occasionally. I've had one on my Suburban do that. Fortunately, Fiero thermostats are simple and fast to replace.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||01-23-2000||02:55 AM||||Dang Pat, somehow you got your post in ahead of me. Great minds think alike.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick||01-23-2000||03:02 AM||||<BR>That's probably true. Now if I only had a V8 in my Fiero... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000006||Phil||01-23-2000||06:21 AM||||Don't they still sell V8 in those little cans? I would think you could fit one of those in there somewhere.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Uaana||01-23-2000||08:05 AM||||Duh Duh.. Smacking self in head..<BR>after replacing about 15 of those things over the years ya think I woulda known....<P>Thanks alot guy.. and is it really as easy as the wonderful gentlemen at Haynes make it sound?||||reg||6
Z||000008||fiero56||01-23-2000||08:49 AM||||Sure is easy. Just pull off the thermostat housing cap, and theres the thermostat. Pull it out and put in a new one. Thats all there is to it. Haynes got something right?||||reg||10
Z||000009||Monkeyman||01-23-2000||12:18 PM||||There's a 1st for everything.||||reg||1
Z||000010||William Federle||01-23-2000||01:00 PM||||I've got a temperature question and I'll ask it here since that's the subject of this thread: How hot is too hot to run an Iron duke? When I got the car, the o-ring around the thermostat leaked and the car never got very warm. I put a new 195 deg thermostat in it and it warmed up much faster and it ran at over 200 deg. I think. The gas mileage did<BR>seem to go up though. Then I put a 180 deg thermostat in it and I can tell that it isn't getting nearly as hot. Also, the gas mileage has dropped. I'd like to put the 195 deg thermostat back in but I don't want to make the engine die an early death. Any ideas?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||ChadMan||01-24-2000||10:00 AM||||Hey Uaana, a quick word of caution:  Replacing the thermostat really is easy, but if the cap hasn't been off the car in quite a while, it may leak when you reinstall it due to corrosion at the top of the thermostat housing!  If it does leak, take some sandpaper and lightly sand the top of the thermostat housing to get rid of any corrosion, so the cap makes a good seal.  Voice of experience talking!||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||01-24-2000||08:44 PM||||200-215 depending on a few things is about noromal for a cast iron motor with a 195 thermostat.||||reg||1
Z||000013||WisconsinGT||01-24-2000||10:12 PM||||Just for the record...I took out my 180 stat and put in a 195. The gas milage is better. I think there is a noticeable difference.<P>On another note. I took my 88GT into the Pontiac dealer today. Had them put in a new fuel filter. Big difference in throttle responce. Yea! But I had them check out a problem I have been having for quite some time. The GT misses at idle. This is what they told me...."Engine has missfire on No. 2 cylinder. Performed cylinder balance test." I guess it could be injectors or possible valve problem. Anyone have any other ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Uaana||01-25-2000||01:14 AM||||Just in case anyone was interested..<BR>Replaced the thermostat today...Worked on that darn cap for about an hour and couldnt get it to budge.. Brought to my regular mech<BR>so he could wrench it off, that took almost half an hour.. And guess what we found inside..NADA.. no thermostat at all!!! Oh well put the new one in and worked for another 15 getting the cap back on.. the rubber must be gettin a little old and not compressing properly.<P>The adventures of buying a rebuilt wreck..<BR>still love her though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||8
Z||000015||Patrick||01-25-2000||03:27 AM||||<BR>Yeah, I guess that would explain why she took a while to warm up...||||reg||10
